TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report says RAAN's initial value indicates the launch facility location. RAAN defines the inertial orientation of the ascending node; it is not determined by the launch site's location alone."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report calls the orbit retrograde but later describes its paths as near-polar without giving a selected local solar time or node-time requirement. A sun-synchronous orbit provides a consistent local solar time, but does not by itself guarantee the claimed imaging illumination conditions."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The claim that a near-polar orbit gives significant contact for the vast majority of passes is not established. Access depends on station latitude, geometry, elevation mask, and orbit; the listed access windows instead show intermittent contacts."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The text claims an average contact duration of almost ten minutes, while the stated mean is 580.786 seconds, approximately 9.68 minutes. The table also has inconsistent access numbering and reports only a subset of the access periods."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The 600 km altitude and 250 km reef width are not connected to a sensor field of view, ground sampling distance, or imaging performance calculation, so the proposed coverage and resolution trade is unsupported."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The five-year lifetime is asserted without spacecraft reliability, power, propellant, or orbital-decay analysis. A drag sail is a plausible passive disposal concept, but its deployment and compliance with applicable disposal limits are not demonstrated."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Vega E is presented as a planned option for a 2025 launch despite being a future launcher in the report's timeframe; the proposed rideshare, orbit compatibility, and launch date are not confirmed."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Reference [6] cites Wikipedia, which the assignment explicitly disallows; several other entries are incomplete and do not provide consistent author, title, date, or access information."}],"overall":"A coherent preliminary concept with useful STK access output and a reasonable single-satellite rationale, but key operational and orbital claims need correction or substantiation. The absent software files, uncertain launch plan, incomplete user definition, and weak sourcing limit confidence in the design.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report explains the coral-monitoring mission, its public and scientific value, and why one spacecraft is proposed. It identifies likely customers but does not clearly identify the spacecraft operator or establish a specific operating arrangement; the rationale and supporting references are also uneven.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM)"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report covers launch, an SSO orbit with several elements, STK access results, a ground facility, mission duration, and a passive drag-sail disposal concept. However, important choices are speculative or insufficiently justified, the orbital description has errors and omissions, and there is no clear validated coverage analysis or supplied software project file. Communication-rate claims are not reconciled with the access results.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the major design choices and acknowledges launch uncertainty, but it mostly restates the proposal. It does not assess whether the evidence demonstrates mission performance or resolve key limitations such as revisit, imaging, and data-transfer capability.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognisable structure, an executive summary, labelled visual material, and a bibliography. Presentation is weakened by grammatical errors, inconsistent figure/table labelling and formatting, incomplete reference details, and use of Wikipedia despite the task explicitly disallowing it. Several claims and design decisions are not adequately cited.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
